Topic: What is your role in the group and has it changed over the semester? If there were any challenges, how did you deal with them? 

By Week 11, my role had shifted towards refining our findings for the final presentation. I reworked some visualisations, such as replacing a scatterplot that showed no meaningful patterns with a table that allowed for more direct quantitative comparisons. Our group also rehearsed together and adjusted our scripts after initially exceeding the time limit.
During this process, I felt challenged when my visualisations did not communicate the intended findings. However, this encouraged me to explore alternative methods, and I felt more confident after making the adjustments.
My evaluation is that our group demonstrated good teamwork, particularly in our willingness to consider feedback and make adjustments. However, we could have been more selective in including essential information, as some sections were initially too detailed to present effectively within the time limit.
My analysis is that my role has developed beyond conducting EDA to considering how findings should be communicated to different audiences. The scatterplot was less effective because the relationships were difficult to distinguish visually, whereas the table allowed for more precise comparisons. This is particularly relevant in real-world applications, where clients may not have the technical knowledge to interpret complicated visualisations. Hence, I realised that the presentation method should depend on the nature of the findings and the audience's needs.
In conclusion, I learned that effective data analysis requires judgement beyond technical accuracy. I could have considered the audience's perspective earlier when selecting visualisations, rather than refining them only during presentation preparation.
Moving forward, I will apply this understanding to our final report by reviewing whether each visualisation contributes meaningfully to the research questions. I will also ensure that our interpretations explain the implications of the findings without unnecessary detail.

